WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
Bismillah wa alhamdulillah wa salatu
wa salam ala rasulullah,

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.470
hello calculus students, today I'll be
showing you how to take derivatives of

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.120
exponential functions. Let's get into it. Let's get
right into our examples. If you guys

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:15.080
do not know the property for when
y equals a to the x,

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:20.100
y prime equals a to the x times ln
a. So it's very close,

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.740
similar to what e was, now the only difference
is when we have y equals a to the x,

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:30.280
the derivative is going to be a to the x,
multiplied by a factor. This factor is ln

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.700
a, or ln whatever number you have. So
here, if we have y equals 5 to

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.970
the x, there should be 5 to the x,
then we have y prime equals

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.610
5 [to the] x times ln of 5.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.810
Let's go [to] this one, and let's do this with
Leibniz notation. Here, if we have y

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:56.250
equals 3 [to the] 2x squared minus 7,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.770
then if we do dy over dx,
here it would be d

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.020
of 3 [to the] 2x squared minus 7, all over d

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:11.650
of 2x squared minus 7,

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.390
multiplied by d of 2x squared

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.800
minus 7 over dx, our chain
rule, which we were

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.160
using when we were taking the derivatives of e
to the x as well, and even when we

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:30.380
were taking the derivatives of ln. Here we
will get 3 [to the] 2x squared minus

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:35.780
7 multiplied by ln of 3 multiplied by 4x.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.080
Here the negative 7 is a
constant, so it goes to 0.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.370
So this, you could keep it like that, or you
could write it just with brackets. I'm

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.410
going to write it with brackets. I'm going to
keep the ln 3 in front, and I'm going

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.330
to go bracket 3 [to the] 2x squared minus 7,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.940
4x.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:02:00.740
On to our third example. Here we have x squared
times 2 to the power of x. So we are

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.480
going to do our product rule, our product rule
tells us when we have two functions

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.840
multiplied together, their derivative is
the same as the first function's

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:11.640
derivative, which is 2x, multiplied by the
second function, which is 2 to the x,

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:16.860
plus the first function, which is x squared,
multiplied by the second function's

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.940
derivative, which is 2 to the x, log of 2.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:26.820
Here, if we want to factor out
an x [times] 2 to the power

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.660
of x, we can take that out, x [times] 2 to the power
of x, and we will be left with 2

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:36.930
plus x ln of 2 as our final

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:37.290
answer.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.420
Lastly here, we have, this is all obviously y
prime, I should have included that.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:48.500
Lastly, let's do y equals 8 to the power
of ln 2x to the power of 3.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.860
So there's a lot of powers here, and I want to
use Leibniz notation to break it

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:56.880
down. So dy over dx here will

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:03.580
be d of 8 [to the] ln of 2x

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.960
cubed all over ln of 2x cubed. This is d,

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.830
of course. They're going to multiply it by the
inside function. The inside function

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.610
here is ln 2x cubed, d of ln

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:24.100
2x cubed. And this is over 2x

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:29.400
cubed, so we're going to have one more
multiplication, d of 2x

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:34.500
cubed all over d of x. We have a
triple chain rule.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.230
So for our first one here, we know all that's
going to happen is we're going to keep

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.670
this 8 [to the] ln 2x cubed multiplied by ln of

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:50.120
8, and then for our second term
we will have just 1

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:55.620
over 2 x cubed because that's what happened
when we take the derivative of ln

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.920
and then for our third we have 2 x cubed
that's 2 times the power rule on x

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:05.900
cubed which is 6 x squared multiplying

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:11.380
all of these together we can do some reducing
here we can get this 2 with this 6

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:16.800
which will give us 3 this [x cubed] with this
x squared, so we'll have a 1x

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:21.840
left over here, a 3 left over there.
So we can have 3 ln 8

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:27.260
multiplied by 8 [to the] ln 2x

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.250
cubed, all over x as our final answer.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:38.060
See, it's not that difficult. It's pretty
similar to our exponent e to the x case.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:43.600
All that we have to do now is add that factor
of ln of whatever number we are

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.740
taking the derivative of. I will
see you in the next one.
